import "forge-std/Test.sol";

// Enjin "Crypto Items" (ERC-1155, ENJ-backed) platform drain — ~5.24M ENJ (~$142K) pulled out of
// ~52 unrelated holder wallets with no approval, then melted for the ENJ backing held in the reserve.
//
// Attacker EOA:            0x5ec1BA7892D11059c39557b762a97DD695778Ca5
// Attacker exploit contract 0x7083DdecE38216C7741fa76c75326Bea744ED321 (deployed nonce 0, same block)
// Platform (ERC-1155 proxy) 0xfaaFDc07907ff5120a76b34b731b278c38d6043C -> impl 0x68Ee930eA6AD962205F1e29AE79BCC3DfA07C837
// Platform storage/reserve  0x4E643a25a64952895f553f20252861258727174e (holds the ENJ backing)
// ENJ token                 0xF629cBd94d3791C9250152BD8dfBDF380E2a3B9c
// Enjin contract registries 0x13fA4b9a6C2F2604C919f96F456e3b50E968b157 and
//                           0x268C039A3127D3107c014F0DC6c390A53e6dB27f (Enjin infra, deployed 2021 by 0x1952e45D)
// Malicious impls deployed   0x99294e5e8dd62Fa0092A85AB37e8B5c44Ec29758 (stealNFT / transferFrom body)
//
// Root cause (verified from the on-chain trace of tx 0xd4a382da..., not just the third-party alert):
//   Enjin's per-item ERC-1155 "adapter" is a delegatecall proxy: each item spawns an adapter clone
//   whose function bodies are looked up from an Enjin "contract registry" via delegates(selector) ->
//   implementation. Those two registries (0x13fA4b9a / 0x268C039A) carry an UNPROTECTED initialize():
//   a fresh, unrelated caller re-ran initialize(1) and then acceptManager(), and the registry emitted
//   ManagerUpdate(oldManager 0x1952e45D -> attacker's exploit contract). i.e. manager takeover of live
//   Enjin infrastructure, reachable by ANY caller. As manager the attacker called
//   updateContract(malImpl, "stealNFT(address,address,uint256);", "pwn721") and
//   updateContract(malImpl, "transferFrom(address,address,uint256);", "pwn20"), registering attacker
//   code as the body those item adapters delegatecall into. The malicious body performs the ERC-1155
//   ledger move with NO owner-approval check, so adapter.stealNFT(holder, attacker, itemId) pulls items
//   straight out of ~52 holders (real TransferSingle events, zero setApprovalForAll anywhere). The
//   attacker then called melt() on each stolen item, redeeming the ~500-ENJ-per-item backing out of the
//   reserve. Every step (registry re-init, manager accept, impl registration, transfer, melt) is
//   permissionless — no privileged key the attacker did not obtain through the re-init bug itself.
//
//   So the Defimon/Decurity framing ("malicious transfer adapter that skips the approval check") is
//   directionally correct; the ENABLING flaw traced here is the unprotected registry initialize() that
//   let any caller become registry manager and install that adapter body. It is fully attacker-controlled.
//
// Self-contained: the exploit contract is deployed (attacker nonce 0) and invoked (nonce 1) in the SAME
// block 25834071. No prior-day setup. This PoC forks one block earlier, redeploys the attacker contract
// at its exact address (attacker nonce 0 -> 0x7083...), and replays the verbatim on-chain calldata.
//
// Run: forge test --contracts ./src/test/2026-08/EnjinCryptoItems_exp.sol -vvv
//      (an inline forge-config selects evm_version=cancun; the malicious impl bytecode needs it)
// Requires an Ethereum archive RPC (mainnet alias in foundry.toml).

